{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 17,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.479,
            0.069,
            0.815,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "Measuring Space: Perimeter and Area"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.844,
            0.068,
            0.884,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": "135"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.107,
            0.884,
            0.169
          ],
          "content": "All three sides have length a, so the semi-perimeter is $s = \\frac{1}{2}(a + a + a) = \\frac{3}{2}a$ units. Heron’s formula now yields,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.207,
            0.165,
            0.792,
            0.215
          ],
          "content": "area of triangle $\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}a\\left(\\frac{1}{2}a\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}a\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}a\\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}a^{2}$ sq. units"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.222,
            0.426,
            0.44
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.237,
                0.447,
                0.316,
                0.466
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.23"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_17_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.438,
            0.223,
            0.877,
            0.302
          ],
          "content": "Let us check this against the ‘half base times height’ formula. Let h units be the height of the triangle. Then, by the Baudhāyana–Pythagoras theorem,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.448,
            0.31,
            0.866,
            0.356
          ],
          "content": "a^{2}-h^{2}=\\frac{a^{2}}{4}, \\quad \\therefore h^{2}=\\frac{3a^{2}}{4}, \\quad \\therefore h=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}a",
          "image_source": "images/page_17_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.438,
            0.365,
            0.875,
            0.435
          ],
          "content": "So the area is $\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}a \\times a = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}a^{2}$ sq. units. Same formula!"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.487,
            0.885,
            0.528
          ],
          "content": "**Note:** Notice the symbol $\\therefore$ . This symbol is used regularly by mathematicians; it means and is read as ‘therefore’."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.539,
            0.883,
            0.561
          ],
          "content": "**Example 4:** An isosceles triangle with equal sides a units and base 2b units."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.565,
            0.883,
            0.613
          ],
          "content": "The semi-perimeter is $\\frac{1}{2}(a + a + 2b) = a + b$ units. Heron’s formula now yields,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.62,
            0.872,
            0.665
          ],
          "content": "area of the triangle $e = \\sqrt{(a + b)(a + b - a)(a + b - a)(a + b - 2b)} = b\\sqrt{a^{2} - b^{2}}$ sq. units."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.671,
            0.415,
            0.855
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.232,
                0.863,
                0.31,
                0.881
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.24"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_17_image_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.425,
            0.682,
            0.877,
            0.762
          ],
          "content": "Let us check this against the ‘half base times height’ formula. Let h units be the height of the triangle. Then, by the Baudhāyana–Pythagoras theorem,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.422,
            0.773,
            0.877,
            0.801
          ],
          "content": "a^{2}-h^{2}=b^{2},\\quad \\therefore h^{2}=a^{2}-b^{2},h=\\sqrt{a^{2}-b^{2}}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_17_equation_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.426,
            0.817,
            0.87,
            0.878
          ],
          "content": "So the area is $\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\sqrt{a^{2} - b^{2}} \\times 2b = b\\sqrt{a^{2} - b^{2}}$ sq. units, the same as earlier."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
